    stem: "Case study: The Plantation of Laois/Offaly",
    questionText: "Taken together, what are the strengths of the documents as historical sources? Refer to both documents in your answer.",
    tariffModel: {"kind":"questionTotal"}, totalMarks: 10,
    rows: [
    { id: "r-1", kind: "point", verbatim: "Taken together, the strengths of the documents include: Both are extracts from the Calendar of State Papers for the Tudor period in Ireland and offer valuable insights into official policy in Ireland in the 1550s", marks: null },
    { id: "r-2", kind: "point", verbatim: "These two letters form part of a direct correspondence between key figures who have responsibility for the government of Ireland", marks: null },
    { id: "r-3", kind: "point", verbatim: "Lord Deputy James Croft’s views are based on his experiences in Ireland, whereas the Privy Council letter lets us see how London responds to these developments", marks: null },
    { id: "r-4", kind: "point", verbatim: "Both letters are primary sources for the period", marks: null },
    { id: "r-5", kind: "point", verbatim: "Both letters cover the same topics, such as what kind of leases to offer, the necessity for defence, the expense of establishing a plantation, etc., and let us see how government policy formed/evolved in response to events as they occurred", marks: null },
    { id: "r-6", kind: "point", verbatim: "The letters show that the Privy Council took account of the Lord Deputy’s advice regarding leases and changed its policy to respond effectively to existing conditions", marks: null },
    { id: "r-7", kind: "point", verbatim: "The five month gap between the letters illustrates how slow communications were in the 1550s and gives us an insight into the difficulties Lord Deputy Croft experienced as he awaited instructions from London", marks: null },
    { id: "r-8", kind: "point", verbatim: "The fact that he sought approval to change the type of leases on offer shows the limitations on his power in Ireland.", marks: null },
    ],
